+# IPAQ LAYOUT
+
+*wvkbd-ipaq* is built for 240x320 and 320x240 handheld screens, and scales
+to 480x640 and 640x480. Every row has ten keys of equal width, so a key is
+24 px wide on a 240 px screen. There are five rows: a function row (*Esc*,
+*Tab*, *Ctl*, *Alt*, the four arrow keys and *Cap* for caps lock) and four rows
+of letters, ending with *Shift*, *Enter*, a layer key on each side of the space
+bar and backspace.
+
+The layers are the letters (Shift gives the capitals; the comma gives ! and the full
+stop ?), *123* for digits and symbols (Shift gives the symbols above
+the digits, and *Del*, the euro and pound signs and a few more are there too)
+and *äé* for the accented letters of Dutch, German and French. *Abc* goes back
+to the letters.
+
+Unless *-H* or *-L* is given, the keyboard is 100 px high on a screen up to
+399 px wide in portrait, 80 px in landscape, and from 400 px wide 160 px in
+portrait and 130 px in landscape. The height follows the screen, not the
+width of a docked keyboard.
+
+This layout honours *--dock*, *--ratio* and *--corner-radius*, and *--width*
+together with *--dock*: the rows are divided over whatever width the keyboard
+gets, so the keys narrow with it (to 16 px at the default docked ratio on a
+240 px screen). It shows no key popup and requests no exclusive zone unless
+*--popup* or *--exclusive* is given. With the popup off its surface, its
+positioner and its two buffers (384 kB at 240x100) are never made. Labels are
+drawn with the 6x13 font on QVGA and 10x20 on VGA screens in the default text
+build.
+
 # COMPOSE BUTTON
 
